WEBVTT

NOTE Episode: Bramble Comes Empty-Handed

1
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:25.200
<v Old Timber>The last trumpet note skipped across the pond. Come close, little ones. I saved you a place, though a frog has placed one elbow in it.</v>

2
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:32.150
<v Old Timber>Have you ever walked into a room and wondered whether people were glad to see you, or only glad about the thing you might bring?</v>

3
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:45.420
<v Old Timber>A joke. A good mark. A clever answer. A story. Lovely gifts. The Bible teaches that God calls us to care. Is a friend still a gift without them?</v>

4
00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:55.716
<v Old Timber>The day I remember began with Garingo the parrot arranging twelve dry leaves into a program for one returning traveler who had not yet appeared.</v>

5
00:00:56.416 --> 00:01:04.541
<v Garingo>Opening announcement. Pause for gasps. Second announcement, in case the first gasp was too small.</v>

6
00:01:04.891 --> 00:01:11.066
<v Garingo>This leaf goes from the spectacular pile to the very spectacular pile.</v>

7
00:01:11.416 --> 00:01:20.225
<v Garingo>BEHOLD! Our traveler returns from the borderlands, where the wind steals hats and breakfast requires a map!</v>

8
00:01:20.575 --> 00:01:28.116
<v Old Timber>Rex the young river otter sat beside the leaf pile with the serious expression of someone prepared to believe in traveling toast.</v>

9
00:01:28.466 --> 00:01:31.054
<v Rex>Does breakfast move in the borderlands?</v>

10
00:01:31.404 --> 00:01:33.521
<v Garingo>Only the famous breakfasts.</v>

11
00:01:33.871 --> 00:01:36.231
<v Rex>I brought a spoon, just in case.</v>

12
00:01:36.581 --> 00:01:43.901
<v Old Timber>Bongo the bear cub arrived carrying a fallen branch across both shoulders, ready to turn it into audience seating.</v>

13
00:01:44.251 --> 00:01:50.931
<v Bongo>The seating is ready. It is also stuck to my left shoulder, but that feels like a later problem.</v>

14
00:01:51.281 --> 00:01:56.441
<v Old Timber>Zippy the hummingbird zipped above the leaves with a seed-pod timer dangling beneath her.</v>

15
00:01:56.791 --> 00:02:04.431
<v Zippy>I timed the ideal gasp at four seconds. Five becomes wheezing. Three suggests mild interest.</v>

16
00:02:04.781 --> 00:02:10.501
<v Old Timber>Fern the young fawn came softly behind them and looked from the waiting seats to the empty path.</v>

17
00:02:10.851 --> 00:02:16.091
<v Fern>I came to welcome our traveler. I did not know welcoming needed a program.</v>

18
00:02:16.441 --> 00:02:25.251
<v Garingo>Excellent. After our guest tells us how he outwitted the hat-stealing wind, you may present the special spoon.</v>

19
00:02:25.601 --> 00:02:27.561
<v Rex>It has oat mush on it.</v>

20
00:02:27.911 --> 00:02:29.878
<v Garingo>Then it has history.</v>

21
00:02:30.228 --> 00:02:36.268
<v Old Timber>Beyond the fern line, Bramble the traveling hedgehog stopped with one dusty foot still raised.</v>

22
00:02:36.618 --> 00:02:38.118
<v Bramble>Hat-stealing wind?</v>

23
00:02:38.468 --> 00:02:44.389
<v Bramble>My journey had a mild breeze, a straight path, and a beetle who declined conversation.</v>

24
00:02:44.739 --> 00:02:50.432
<v Bramble>The beetle did nod. Or stepped over a seed. Difficult to say.</v>

25
00:02:50.782 --> 00:02:56.682
<v Garingo>Leaf four: Bramble reveals the object he carried through great danger.</v>

26
00:02:57.032 --> 00:02:58.321
<v Rex>What object?</v>

27
00:02:58.671 --> 00:03:01.396
<v Garingo>That is why leaf four is gripping.</v>

28
00:03:01.746 --> 00:03:06.472
<v Bramble>Half an oatcake, two clean socks, and no grand object at all.</v>

29
00:03:06.822 --> 00:03:11.053
<v Bramble>I could wait until tomorrow. Something might happen by then.</v>

30
00:03:11.403 --> 00:03:18.623
<v Old Timber>He had thanked God for friends worth walking to. Now he wondered whether arriving without a tale would let them down.</v>

31
00:03:18.973 --> 00:03:26.884
<v Old Timber>He lowered his foot and began to turn away. The pine cone beside it rolled downhill and tapped Rex on the tail.</v>

32
00:03:27.234 --> 00:03:30.714
<v Rex>A pine cone has brought a message to my tail.</v>

33
00:03:31.064 --> 00:03:33.503
<v Garingo>The object! Places, everyone!</v>

34
00:03:33.853 --> 00:03:37.093
<v Rex>Bramble? I can see your foot behind the fern.</v>

35
00:03:37.443 --> 00:03:39.573
<v Bramble>Only the dusty one, I hope.</v>

36
00:03:39.923 --> 00:03:43.056
<v Rex>Both feet now. And most of a hedgehog.</v>

37
00:03:43.406 --> 00:03:51.126
<v Old Timber>Bramble stepped out. Garingo fanned all twelve leaves around his perch as though the sun had arrived by appointment.</v>

38
00:03:51.476 --> 00:03:56.758
<v Garingo>Welcome, traveler! We are ready for absolutely everything that happened.</v>

39
00:03:57.108 --> 00:03:59.544
<v Bramble>That may be quicker than you think.</v>

40
00:04:00.253 --> 00:04:07.750
<v Garingo>First, the dust upon your foot. Desert storm, runaway cart, or duel with a flour sack?</v>

41
00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.585
<v Bramble>The path was dusty.</v>

42
00:04:09.935 --> 00:04:14.370
<v Garingo>A path so dusty that no further description survived!</v>

43
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.375
<v Rex>I think the description survived. It was dusty.</v>

44
00:04:18.725 --> 00:04:24.557
<v Garingo>Good. We shall call that chapter one: Dust Beyond Description.</v>

45
00:04:24.907 --> 00:04:27.176
<v Bramble>It was mostly beside description.</v>

46
00:04:27.526 --> 00:04:32.805
<v Bramble>Three leaves complete already? Your pile is becoming more exciting than my morning.</v>

47
00:04:33.155 --> 00:04:42.500
<v Garingo>Second, the mysterious pine cone. Did it guide you through fog? Warn you of danger? Defeat the flour sack?</v>

48
00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:44.681
<v Bramble>It rolled against my foot.</v>

49
00:04:45.031 --> 00:04:46.640
<v Garingo>Of its own accord!</v>

50
00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:47.866
<v Rex>Downhill.</v>

51
00:04:48.216 --> 00:04:52.584
<v Garingo>Downhill, where gravity itself had prepared an entrance.</v>

52
00:04:52.934 --> 00:04:55.304
<v Bramble>I picked it up because it was there.</v>

53
00:04:55.654 --> 00:04:58.313
<v Garingo>A simple choice that takes your breath away.</v>

54
00:04:58.663 --> 00:05:01.184
<v Rex>I make that choice with snacks.</v>

55
00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:09.147
<v Old Timber>A slow shell-scrape announced Shelly the young tortoise. She read the upside-down program leaf nearest her.</v>

56
00:05:09.497 --> 00:05:12.497
<v Shelly>It says, 'Thunderous applause, if you have any.'</v>

57
00:05:12.847 --> 00:05:14.015
<v Garingo>Do we have any?</v>

58
00:05:14.365 --> 00:05:18.053
<v Shelly>I can tap one foot. The other three are occupied.</v>

59
00:05:18.403 --> 00:05:22.123
<v Rex>I can clap, but I am still holding the special spoon.</v>

60
00:05:22.473 --> 00:05:25.071
<v Bongo>I can clap once I stop wearing the seating.</v>

61
00:05:25.421 --> 00:05:30.346
<v Zippy>I can cancel the gasp timer. I need a name for no gasp.</v>

62
00:05:30.696 --> 00:05:32.091
<v Bramble>No applause, please.</v>

63
00:05:32.441 --> 00:05:37.061
<v Garingo>Of course. We will use stunned silence. More tasteful.</v>

64
00:05:37.411 --> 00:05:41.531
<v Bramble>Everyone is silent in a way that is very easy to notice.</v>

65
00:05:41.881 --> 00:05:44.998
<v Bramble>That is somehow more applause than applause.</v>

66
00:05:45.348 --> 00:05:47.148
<v Shelly>He asked for less attention.</v>

67
00:05:47.498 --> 00:05:52.354
<v Garingo>Yes. Less. Naturally. I am lowering the occasion now.</v>

68
00:05:52.704 --> 00:05:56.104
<v Shelly>You are lower. The occasion is not.</v>

69
00:05:56.454 --> 00:06:03.044
<v Garingo>Third and finally: the bindle. Bramble, what amazing treasure survived inside it?</v>

70
00:06:03.394 --> 00:06:05.590
<v Bramble>Two socks and half an oatcake.</v>

71
00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:07.721
<v Rex>That is almost lunch.</v>

72
00:06:08.071 --> 00:06:09.069
<v Garingo>And the socks?</v>

73
00:06:09.419 --> 00:06:10.724
<v Bramble>They are socks.</v>

74
00:06:11.074 --> 00:06:12.540
<v Shelly>A strong answer.</v>

75
00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:20.847
<v Garingo>Certainly. But perhaps one sock crossed a raging stream while the other waited faithfully on the bank?</v>

76
00:06:21.197 --> 00:06:23.070
<v Bramble>They were folded together.</v>

77
00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:25.123
<v Rex>Faithful indeed.</v>

78
00:06:25.473 --> 00:06:29.388
<v Bramble>I nearly smiled. Then you lifted the next leaf.</v>

79
00:06:29.738 --> 00:06:30.559
<v Garingo>And then?</v>

80
00:06:30.909 --> 00:06:32.315
<v Bramble>Then I arrived.</v>

81
00:06:32.665 --> 00:06:35.857
<v Garingo>Yes, yes. But before that, what happened?</v>

82
00:06:36.569 --> 00:06:38.835
<v Bramble>A cloud covered the sun for a moment.</v>

83
00:06:39.185 --> 00:06:40.940
<v Garingo>A sudden darkness!</v>

84
00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:42.726
<v Bramble>It was a small cloud.</v>

85
00:06:43.076 --> 00:06:46.153
<v Garingo>Small, but bold. Continue.</v>

86
00:06:46.503 --> 00:06:48.261
<v Bramble>I waited beside a puddle.</v>

87
00:06:48.611 --> 00:06:50.253
<v Garingo>Because it was bottomless?</v>

88
00:06:50.603 --> 00:06:52.618
<v Bramble>Because my sock had slipped.</v>

89
00:06:52.968 --> 00:06:54.608
<v Rex>One of the faithful pair?</v>

90
00:06:54.958 --> 00:06:56.044
<v Bramble>The left one.</v>

91
00:06:56.394 --> 00:07:01.146
<v Garingo>Apart at last! The stream prophecy comes true.</v>

92
00:07:01.496 --> 00:07:02.724
<v Shelly>It was a puddle.</v>

93
00:07:03.074 --> 00:07:04.734
<v Garingo>A compact stream.</v>

94
00:07:05.084 --> 00:07:10.579
<v Bramble>Now two field mice and a wren are watching. Please do not write 'new audience.'</v>

95
00:07:10.929 --> 00:07:13.605
<v Bramble>There was also a sound in the bushes.</v>

96
00:07:13.955 --> 00:07:19.965
<v Garingo>At last! A trumpet? A cartwheel? A hidden drummer with excellent timing?</v>

97
00:07:20.315 --> 00:07:21.555
<v Bramble>Probably the beetle.</v>

98
00:07:21.905 --> 00:07:24.012
<v Rex>The one who did not talk?</v>

99
00:07:24.362 --> 00:07:25.780
<v Bramble>Possibly his cousin.</v>

100
00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:29.406
<v Garingo>Two beetles! A silent family feud!</v>

101
00:07:29.756 --> 00:07:31.177
<v Bramble>No. Stop.</v>

102
00:07:31.527 --> 00:07:35.567
<v Rex>The program leaves just went still in Garingo's claws.</v>

103
00:07:35.917 --> 00:07:41.573
<v Bramble>I do not know whose cousin it was. I do not even know whether it was a beetle.</v>

104
00:07:41.923 --> 00:07:47.716
<v Garingo>Then we can leave the creature mysterious. Audiences appreciate mystery.</v>

105
00:07:48.066 --> 00:07:49.705
<v Bramble>I do not want an audience.</v>

106
00:07:50.055 --> 00:07:56.454
<v Garingo>They are hardly an audience. More of a very small gathering with excellent listening posture.</v>

107
00:07:56.804 --> 00:07:59.003
<v Shelly>That is an audience.</v>

108
00:07:59.353 --> 00:08:02.183
<v Rex>And Bramble is backing toward the ferns.</v>

109
00:08:02.533 --> 00:08:05.361
<v Bramble>My bindle is caught. One moment.</v>

110
00:08:05.711 --> 00:08:08.618
<v Garingo>Wait. We have not reached the great danger.</v>

111
00:08:08.968 --> 00:08:10.168
<v Bramble>There wasn't any.</v>

112
00:08:10.518 --> 00:08:13.790
<v Shelly>The field mice are inspecting the opposite bank now.</v>

113
00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:21.319
<v Bramble>That is the whole story. I left home. I walked here. Nothing difficult happened.</v>

114
00:08:21.669 --> 00:08:24.439
<v Garingo>But you always bring something amazing.</v>

115
00:08:24.789 --> 00:08:25.579
<v Bramble>I know.</v>

116
00:08:25.929 --> 00:08:30.609
<v Old Timber>Bramble stepped through the ferns. Rex set down the spoon and followed him.</v>

117
00:08:30.959 --> 00:08:35.877
<v Old Timber>Garingo remained beside twelve leaves with no ending written on any of them.</v>

118
00:08:36.585 --> 00:08:41.421
<v Old Timber>Rex found Bramble beneath a cedar, tightening a bindle that was already tight.</v>

119
00:08:41.771 --> 00:08:43.091
<v Rex>Were you going home?</v>

120
00:08:43.441 --> 00:08:44.598
<v Bramble>I thought about it.</v>

121
00:08:44.948 --> 00:08:46.428
<v Rex>Before we saw you?</v>

122
00:08:46.778 --> 00:08:48.525
<v Bramble>Before I could let you down.</v>

123
00:08:48.875 --> 00:08:51.250
<v Rex>I was not waiting for a story.</v>

124
00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:58.798
<v Bramble>Garingo was. The leaves were. Everyone leaned forward before I had said hello.</v>

125
00:08:59.148 --> 00:09:06.545
<v Bramble>At every new place, a good story gives me a door. People open it, and I know how to walk in.</v>

126
00:09:06.895 --> 00:09:13.181
<v Bramble>Today I had no story. I thought if I had nothing to tell, I had nothing to bring.</v>

127
00:09:13.531 --> 00:09:15.011
<v Rex>You brought Bramble.</v>

128
00:09:15.361 --> 00:09:19.873
<v Bramble>I am trying to believe that is enough when nobody is clapping for it.</v>

129
00:09:20.223 --> 00:09:25.851
<v Rex>I cannot prove it with a cheer. I can stay while you decide whether you believe it.</v>

130
00:09:26.201 --> 00:09:33.121
<v Old Timber>A dry leaf shifted behind them. Garingo had followed, but he did not step into the clearing.</v>

131
00:09:33.471 --> 00:09:34.947
<v Garingo>May I come closer?</v>

132
00:09:35.297 --> 00:09:36.097
<v Bramble>Yes.</v>

133
00:09:36.447 --> 00:09:44.327
<v Old Timber>Garingo came without the program. He perched on a low root far enough away that Bramble did not have to make room.</v>

134
00:09:44.677 --> 00:09:46.343
<v Garingo>I thought I was honoring you.</v>

135
00:09:46.693 --> 00:09:49.002
<v Bramble>You were honoring what I usually do.</v>

136
00:09:49.352 --> 00:09:52.703
<v Garingo>And I kept asking for it after you asked me to stop.</v>

137
00:09:53.053 --> 00:09:53.905
<v Bramble>Yes.</v>

138
00:09:54.255 --> 00:09:58.513
<v Garingo>I made your hello feel like the small part before the important part.</v>

139
00:09:58.863 --> 00:10:05.609
<v Bramble>I love telling stories. I was afraid that if the stories stopped, the welcome would stop too.</v>

140
00:10:05.959 --> 00:10:11.106
<v Garingo>Your stories are something I love about you. They are not the reason I love you.</v>

141
00:10:11.456 --> 00:10:13.156
<v Bramble>I needed to hear that.</v>

142
00:10:13.506 --> 00:10:19.126
<v Garingo>The mice, the wren, and Shelly are still waiting beside my leaf piles.</v>

143
00:10:19.476 --> 00:10:23.888
<v Old Timber>By then I had reached the cedar. I kept to the edge of the shade.</v>

144
00:10:24.238 --> 00:10:31.798
<v Old Timber>The Bible has a line about honoring one another. Garingo, what would it mean to honor the friend in front of you now?</v>

145
00:10:32.148 --> 00:10:39.748
<v Garingo>God calls me to honor Bramble, not my show. It means ending the event, even though I wanted it.</v>

146
00:10:40.098 --> 00:10:46.708
<v Garingo>I want to fix this with a speech. That is probably the same problem wearing a clean hat.</v>

147
00:10:47.058 --> 00:10:49.153
<v Rex>A very clean hat.</v>

148
00:10:49.503 --> 00:10:58.446
<v Garingo>Then I will use one sentence. Bramble, I am sorry I treated your friendship like the opening act for your stories.</v>

149
00:10:58.796 --> 00:10:59.629
<v Bramble>Thank you.</v>

150
00:10:59.979 --> 00:11:08.022
<v Garingo>I am going to end the event. After that, would you let me spend the day with you without asking what happens next?</v>

151
00:11:08.372 --> 00:11:13.784
<v Bramble>You may ask what happens next if you can accept the answer 'lunch.'</v>

152
00:11:14.134 --> 00:11:16.174
<v Rex>I left a spoon in the event.</v>

153
00:11:16.524 --> 00:11:19.040
<v Garingo>Then lunch already has equipment.</v>

154
00:11:19.752 --> 00:11:24.672
<v Old Timber>Garingo returned to the pond and lifted the first program leaf in his beak.</v>

155
00:11:25.022 --> 00:11:32.134
<v Garingo>Friends, there will be no grand tale today. I asked Bramble to perform when he had come to visit.</v>

156
00:11:32.484 --> 00:11:37.575
<v Garingo>Please go enjoy your own mornings. Bramble does not owe us an adventure.</v>

157
00:11:37.925 --> 00:11:41.725
<v Fern>Bramble, would you like space, or would quiet company help?</v>

158
00:11:42.075 --> 00:11:45.765
<v Bramble>Quiet company would help, if no one waits for a story.</v>

159
00:11:46.115 --> 00:11:50.352
<v Bongo>Understood. I will return the seating to being a branch.</v>

160
00:11:50.702 --> 00:11:58.284
<v Zippy>Gasp timer deleted. I am keeping the seed pod because it is also a seed pod.</v>

161
00:11:58.634 --> 00:11:59.474
<v Shelly>Understood.</v>

162
00:11:59.824 --> 00:12:07.210
<v Old Timber>The mice left. The wren flew off. Fern turned the applause leaf into a shade roof for a ladybird.</v>

163
00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:10.505
<v Garingo>Leaf four has found better work.</v>

164
00:12:10.855 --> 00:12:14.975
<v Old Timber>He folded the remaining program twice and tucked it beneath a stone.</v>

165
00:12:15.325 --> 00:12:21.806
<v Garingo>No grand openings. No chapters. What would an ordinary day with Bramble include?</v>

166
00:12:22.156 --> 00:12:24.301
<v Bramble>That stepping stone is leaning.</v>

167
00:12:24.651 --> 00:12:28.131
<v Rex>I noticed it yesterday and decided it was practicing.</v>

168
00:12:28.481 --> 00:12:30.281
<v Shelly>It has practiced enough.</v>

169
00:12:30.631 --> 00:12:39.551
<v Bongo>I can lift the heavy side slowly. Slowly matters because Shelly is standing on the other side.</v>

170
00:12:39.901 --> 00:12:49.781
<v Zippy>I will call level. Left a little. Right a speck. Stop. That is exactly one stone of level.</v>

171
00:12:50.131 --> 00:12:56.799
<v Rex>The stone moved! The twig moved too. The mud is now on Garingo.</v>

172
00:12:57.149 --> 00:13:00.149
<v Rex>Level stones! New favorite thing!</v>

173
00:13:00.499 --> 00:13:03.558
<v Garingo>The mud has accepted my feathers as a painting.</v>

174
00:13:03.908 --> 00:13:06.237
<v Bramble>Wipe upward, without the drama.</v>

175
00:13:06.587 --> 00:13:09.196
<v Garingo>Those directions disagree with my training.</v>

176
00:13:09.546 --> 00:13:13.876
<v Rex>I can wipe without drama. I have no training at all.</v>

177
00:13:14.226 --> 00:13:19.103
<v Garingo>My feathers are greenish-brown, and my dignity is ready for lunch.</v>

178
00:13:19.453 --> 00:13:21.743
<v Bramble>Half an oatcake, four friends.</v>

179
00:13:22.093 --> 00:13:24.303
<v Shelly>That is one eighth each.</v>

180
00:13:24.653 --> 00:13:27.636
<v Rex>I prefer the phrase 'a brave crumb.'</v>

181
00:13:27.986 --> 00:13:32.282
<v Garingo>I have three mango slices in a leaf packet under my wing.</v>

182
00:13:32.632 --> 00:13:34.957
<v Bramble>You carried mangoes under your wing?</v>

183
00:13:35.307 --> 00:13:39.534
<v Garingo>Today they are only fruit. No legend is attached.</v>

184
00:13:39.884 --> 00:13:45.795
<v Bramble>The crumbs are gone, the mango is gone, and here comes a short rain on the leaves.</v>

185
00:13:46.145 --> 00:13:48.176
<v Bramble>We could shelter under the cedar.</v>

186
00:13:48.526 --> 00:13:49.497
<v Garingo>Lead the way.</v>

187
00:13:49.847 --> 00:13:55.027
<v Bramble>Under the cedar, this pine cone has closed its scales in the damp air.</v>

188
00:13:55.377 --> 00:13:58.457
<v Rex>It looks as though it is keeping a secret.</v>

189
00:13:58.807 --> 00:14:04.196
<v Bramble>Or responding to rain in the way pine cones do. We can ask Hoot later.</v>

190
00:14:04.546 --> 00:14:07.606
<v Garingo>I have seven possible titles for that fact.</v>

191
00:14:07.956 --> 00:14:09.571
<v Bramble>Keep them in your head.</v>

192
00:14:09.921 --> 00:14:12.597
<v Garingo>I will keep six. One escaped.</v>

193
00:14:12.947 --> 00:14:16.507
<v Shelly>Was it 'The Cone That Closed Against the Sky'?</v>

194
00:14:16.857 --> 00:14:17.780
<v Garingo>It was.</v>

195
00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:28.250
<v Old Timber>The shower passed. No danger arrived. No crowd returned. For a while, four friends listened to drops leave the cedar tips.</v>

196
00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:32.883
<v Bramble>This is the part I usually hurry past when I tell a story.</v>

197
00:14:33.233 --> 00:14:35.078
<v Rex>Because nothing is happening?</v>

198
00:14:35.428 --> 00:14:40.844
<v Bramble>Because I was with all of you, and I did not have to make the moment bigger.</v>

199
00:14:41.194 --> 00:14:46.711
<v Garingo>And I nearly missed the rain, the stone, and lunch because I was waiting for thunder.</v>

200
00:14:47.061 --> 00:14:50.719
<v Bramble>I came with half an oatcake, two socks, and myself.</v>

201
00:14:51.069 --> 00:14:53.108
<v Rex>The socks were faithful.</v>

202
00:14:53.458 --> 00:14:55.537
<v Bramble>They had an excellent day.</v>

203
00:14:56.251 --> 00:15:03.251
<v Old Timber>Back at the pond, Rex turned the special spoon over in his paws and remembered the choice Garingo had made.</v>

204
00:15:03.601 --> 00:15:07.267
<v Rex>Why did ending the event honor Bramble more than finishing it?</v>

205
00:15:07.617 --> 00:15:15.233
<v Garingo>Because I finally put what Bramble needed ahead of the show I wanted. That is what honor looked like in my claws.</v>

206
00:15:15.583 --> 00:15:20.353
<v Bramble>No one asked me for another tale. They simply stayed, and I could stay too.</v>

207
00:15:20.703 --> 00:15:30.182
<v Rex>A friend can bring a joke, a trophy, a good mark, or a story. But those are gifts from the friend. They are not the friend.</v>

208
00:15:30.532 --> 00:15:34.542
<v Shelly>Honor listens when someone says, 'Please stop.'</v>

209
00:15:34.892 --> 00:15:37.067
<v Rex>That is what changed the day.</v>

210
00:15:37.417 --> 00:15:45.777
<v Old Timber>Paul wrote these words to Christians in Rome. They are in the Bible, Romans chapter twelve, verse ten, in the King James Version.</v>

211
00:15:46.127 --> 00:15:52.186
<v Old Timber>Be kindly affectioned one to another with brotherly love; in honour preferring one another;</v>

212
00:15:52.536 --> 00:15:53.839
<v Rex>What's that mean?</v>

213
00:15:54.189 --> 00:16:02.149
<v Shelly>It means: care for one another like family, and be ready to put another person's good before your own show.</v>

214
00:16:02.499 --> 00:16:08.959
<v Garingo>Preferring Bramble meant ending my event, even though I had prepared twelve excellent leaves.</v>

215
00:16:09.309 --> 00:16:13.149
<v Bramble>Eleven excellent leaves. One was upside down.</v>

216
00:16:13.499 --> 00:16:16.899
<v Shelly>The upside-down one did useful work for a ladybird.</v>

217
00:16:17.249 --> 00:16:19.529
<v Old Timber>A welcome like that is worth remembering.</v>

218
00:16:20.232 --> 00:16:28.992
<v Old Timber>Round eyes appeared above the closed pine cone. They belonged to Professor Hoot, our owl, who had brought one carefully checked fact.</v>

219
00:16:29.342 --> 00:16:35.105
<v Professor Hoot>Observe the cone, children. Its scales can respond to humidity.</v>

220
00:16:35.455 --> 00:16:36.855
<v Rex>What's that mean?</v>

221
00:16:37.205 --> 00:16:47.316
<v Professor Hoot>Moisture in the air is what the word means. In dry air, many seed-bearing pine cones open their scales. In damp air, they close.</v>

222
00:16:47.666 --> 00:16:54.484
<v Professor Hoot>No thought or decision is required. The scales simply respond when the air becomes wetter or drier.</v>

223
00:16:54.834 --> 00:16:57.030
<v Garingo>Only six titles.</v>

224
00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:02.140
<v Old Timber>A true fact and six titles in one parrot's head. Thank you, Professor.</v>

225
00:17:07.992 --> 00:17:17.632
<v Old Timber>Three splashes arrived from three directions. Splish, Splash, and Splosh, the three frog brothers, had begun Mudpuppy Minutes.</v>

226
00:17:17.982 --> 00:17:22.182
<v Splosh>Today on Mudpuppy Minutes: why do onions make your eyes water?</v>

227
00:17:22.532 --> 00:17:26.713
<v Splish>Because every onion is telling a sad story about being left out of the soup.</v>

228
00:17:27.063 --> 00:17:33.952
<v Splash>Incorrect! An onion is a tiny weather machine, and your eyes forgot their umbrellas.</v>

229
00:17:34.302 --> 00:17:41.942
<v Splosh>Or cutting an onion releases something into the air that bothers our eyes, and tears help wash it away.</v>

230
00:17:42.292 --> 00:17:46.803
<v Splish>Oh no. That is far too useful. Onions do not own the air.</v>

231
00:17:47.153 --> 00:17:51.272
<v Splash>And tears cannot wash anything. They have no hands.</v>

232
00:17:51.622 --> 00:17:56.855
<v Splosh>Then the onion must whistle a very sad note that only eyeballs can hear.</v>

233
00:17:57.205 --> 00:18:00.205
<v Splish>My left eyeball says the note is in soup minor.</v>

234
00:18:00.555 --> 00:18:03.545
<v Splash>Mine says the onion needs a smaller handkerchief.</v>

235
00:18:03.895 --> 00:18:09.668
<v Splosh>Mudpuppies, mudpuppies, we have solved it. Onion tears are weather for eyeballs.</v>

236
00:18:10.018 --> 00:18:11.231
<v Splish>Should we tell the soup?</v>

237
00:18:11.581 --> 00:18:14.220
<v Splash>Too late. It is already wearing boots.</v>

238
00:18:14.570 --> 00:18:22.649
<v Old Timber>Cutting an onion releases chemicals into the air that can bother your eyes, so your eyes make tears to protect and rinse themselves.</v>

239
00:18:22.999 --> 00:18:29.039
<v Old Timber>No sad soup story, tiny weather machine, handkerchief, or boots are required.</v>

240
00:18:29.744 --> 00:18:36.984
<v Old Timber>A friend is more than the joke, mark, gift, or story they bring. Their quiet company can be a gift too.</v>

241
00:18:37.334 --> 00:18:42.094
<v Old Timber>God's love teaches honor with kindness, including room for a friend's quiet.</v>

242
00:18:42.444 --> 00:18:46.055
<v Bramble>You may come empty-handed. Faithful socks are optional.</v>

243
00:18:46.405 --> 00:18:51.082
<v Old Timber>Until another ordinary day becomes worth sharing, this is Old Timber.</v>

244
00:18:51.432 --> 00:18:54.492
<v Ranger Ray>See you next time, somewhere in Shiloh Creek.</v>
